  "nl_text": "The database schema named 'rschema_0167' consists of five tables: 'natural_disaster_data', 'region_information', 'department', 'user', and 'region_and_department'.\nThe 'natural_disaster_data' table holds data related to natural disasters. It includes columns for data id, data type, data source, upload time, data content, data format, and layer type. The data id is the primary key and is used to uniquely identify each record.\nThe 'region_information' table contains information about various regions. It includes columns for region id, region name, administrative division, latitude and longitude, area, and disaster risk level. The region id is the primary key and is used to identify each region.\nThe 'department' table holds information about various departments. It includes columns for department id, department name, person-in-charge, contact information, and functional scope. The department id is the primary key and is used to identify each department.\nThe 'user' table contains information about users. It includes columns for user id, username, role, contact information, permissions, and department id. The user id is the primary key and is used to identify each user. The department id is a foreign key referencing the department table, establishing a relationship between users and their respective departments.\nFinally, the 'region_and_department' table acts as a junction table to link regions and departments. It includes columns for region id and department id, and also includes a column for division scope. Both region id and department id are foreign keys referencing the 'region_information' and 'department' tables respectively, establishing a relationship between regions and departments. The combination of department id and region id forms the primary key of this table, ensuring uniqueness of the record. The delete and update rules are set to cascade, meaning if a region or department is deleted or updated, the corresponding records in the 'region_and_department' table will also be deleted or updated.",
  "gold_question": "The Jinan City Natural Disaster Comprehensive Risk Data Management System is based on a Browser/Server (B/S) architecture. The system aims to integrate and manage the results of the natural disaster risk survey in Jinan City, achieving efficient data management and multi-level sharing. The main functional requirements of the system include:\n1.Natural Disaster Data Management: The system will record relevant data related to natural disasters, such as Data ID, Data Type, Data Source, Upload Time, Data Content, Data Format, and Layer Type, to analyze natural disaster risks.\n2.Regional Data Management: Each region has Attributes like Region Name, Administrative Division, Latitude and Longitude, Area, and Disaster Risk Level, and each region may contain multiple natural disaster data.\n3.Department Data Management: Each department records Attributes such as Department Name, Person-in-Charge, Contact Information, and Functional Scope. Each region can have multiple departments, and each department can manage multiple regions.\n4.User Management: Each user has a unique User ID, Username, Role, and Contact Information. Each department has multiple users, and each user has a different Permission Level.",
  "input_json": "/data/RSchema_ExpResults4/Experiment2/schema2text_deepseek/0167.json",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: rschema_0167\nCREATE TABLE `natural_disaster_data` (\n  `data_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `data_type` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `data_source` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `upload_time` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `data_content` text NOT NULL,\n  `data_format` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  `layer_type` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`data_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `region_information` (\n  `region_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `region_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `administrative_division` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `latitude` decimal(9,6) NOT NULL,\n  `longitude` decimal(9,6) NOT NULL,\n  `area` decimal(10,2) NOT NULL,\n  `disaster_risk_level` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`region_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `department` (\n  `department_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `department_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `person_in_charge` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `contact_information` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `functional_scope` text NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`department_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `user` (\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `username` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `role` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  `contact_information` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `permissions` text NOT NULL,\n  `department_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`user_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `user_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`department_id`) REFERENCES `department` (`department_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `region_and_department` (\n  `region_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `department_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `division_scope` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`region_id`, `department_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `region_and_department_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`region_id`) REFERENCES `region_information` (`region_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `region_and_department_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`department_id`) REFERENCES `department` (`department_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
